==Old Saxon==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|osx}} {{m|gem-pro|*wurmiz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|osx}} {{m|gem-pro|*wr̥mis}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|wirm}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|wyrm}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|wurm}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Wurm}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|ormr}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|orm||serpent}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌼𐍃||worm, serpent}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|worm}} and {{m|nl|wurm}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|vermis||worm}}, {{etyl|lt|-}} {{m|lt|varmas||midge}}, {{etyl|orv|-}} {{m|orv|вермие||locusts, worms}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|ῥόμος||earthworm}} (originally *{{m|grc||ϝράμος}}).

